<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="chp3sec4" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

        <title>Linear Maps and Basis</title>
        <p>
            In many ways, bases contained the distilled essence of a finite dimensional vector space in a concise, finite collection. In this investigation, you see how you can build linear maps via the basis and how a linear map is completely determined by what it does to a basis.
        </p>
        <proposition xml:id="prop-map_extend_from_basis">
            <title>Extend a map linearly from a map on the basis</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>V</m> is a finite dimensional vector space with ordered basis, <m>\mathcal{B}=\{\vect{v}_1,\ldots, \vect{v}_n\}\subset V</m> , <m>W</m> is a vector space, and <m>T_0:\mathcal{B}\to W</m> is a set map, then there exists a linear map <m>T:V\to W</m> such that for each <m>\vect{v}_i\in \mathcal{B}</m>, <m>T(\vect{v}_i)= T_0(\vect{v}_i)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </proposition>
        <proof>
            <p>
                Try a direct proof. To define a map, you need to say what it does to each element of <m>V</m>. Every element of <m>V</m> is a linear combination of the basis (why???). If <m>\vect{v} = x_1\vect{v}_1+\cdots +x_n\vect{v}_n</m>, define the set map <m>T(\vect{v}) := x_1T_0(\vect{v}_1)+\cdots +x_nT_0(\vect{v}_n)</m>. Show that <m>T</m> is linear and agrees with <m>T_0</m> when restricted to the basis. You might also find <xref ref="def-linearmap"/> and <xref ref="prop-basis-lincomb_exists_unique"/> useful.
            </p>
        </proof>
        <p>
            This process of building a linear map via specifying what it does to a basis is called <em>extending linearly</em>.
        </p>

        <insight>
        <p>
            In calculus, you often analyzed functions that had clean and concise symbolic expressions (e.g. linear, exponential, trigonometric, etc). These symbolic expressions enabled you to communicate your thoughts to others. Now think about what you just proved in <xref ref="prop-map_extend_from_basis"/> - you describe a linear map by describing where the basis is sent.
        </p>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>In your own words, internalize and explain the power of this result for <em>communicating</em> to others what linear map you are working with.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>What alternative ways do you have for communicating a given linear map if not for specifying what it does to a chosen basis? Explain?</p>
            </li>
        </ol>
                </insight>


        <proposition xml:id="prop-map_determined_by_basis">
            <title>A map is uniquely determined by what it does to a basis</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>V</m> is a finite dimensional vector space with ordered basis, <m>\mathcal{B}=\{\vect{v}_1,\ldots, \vect{v}_n\}\subset V</m> , <m>W</m> is a vector space, and <m>T_1,T_2:V\to W</m> are linear maps such that for each <m>\vect{v}_i\in \mathcal{B}</m>, <m>T_1(\vect{v}_i)= T_2(\vect{v}_i)</m>, then <m>T_1=T_2</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </proposition>
        <proof>
            <p>
                Try a direct proof. Two set maps are equal if they agree on all inputs. Pick <m>\vect{v}\in V</m> and show <m>T_1(\vect{v})=T_2(\vect{v})</m>. You might also find <xref ref="def-linearmap"/> and <xref ref="prop-basis-lincomb_exists_unique"/> useful.
            </p>
        </proof>

        <insight>

        <p>
            In this reflection, you will analyze the task of verifying if two set maps are equal. To set maps are equal if they agree on all inputs. Vector spaces are usually quite big - in fact - <q>usually</q> infinite. To verify equality of two set maps would amount to checking infinitely many inputs! Now think about what you just proved in <xref ref="prop-map_determined_by_basis"/> - you need to only check a finite list. In several paragraphs, carefully responds to the following prompts.
        </p>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>In your own words, internalize and explain the power of this result.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>In what other contexts have you seen similar results (e.g. isometries in transformational geometry)? Describe and explain.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>In what ways might you say these kinds of results, in which you <em>reduce infinite into finite</em>, are problem solving strategies? Explain.</p>
            </li>
        </ol>
        </insight>



</section>








